CAT = "ukfoodHazards"; SEG = "HAZ"
Q = [
("Which of these is a physical contamination hazard?",
 "A fragment of glass", ["Bleach on a worktop", "Salmonella on raw chicken", "Peanut traces in a sauce"],
 "Physical hazards are foreign objects such as glass, metal, hair or plasters. Bleach is a chemical hazard, Salmonella is microbiological and peanut is an allergen."),
("Which of these is a chemical contamination hazard?",
 "Cleaning fluid in a sauce", ["A long hair in the soup", "Listeria in chicken pâté", "A blue plaster in a salad"],
 "Cleaning chemicals, pest bait and machine oil are chemical hazards. Hair and plasters are physical, and Listeria is microbiological."),
("Which of these is a microbiological hazard?",
 "Norovirus on a salad", ["A stone in lentils", "Oil from a meat slicer", "A staple in a bun"],
 "Microbiological hazards are bacteria, viruses, moulds and parasites. Stones and staples are physical hazards and machine oil is chemical."),
("What do bacteria need to multiply?",
 "Food, moisture, warmth and time", ["Light, salt, cold and time", "Sugar, acid, air and light", "Food, sunlight, oxygen and salt"],
 "Bacteria multiply when they have food, moisture, warmth and enough time. Taking away any one of these, for example by chilling, slows or stops growth."),
("In ideal conditions, how often can some bacteria divide in two?",
 "Every 10 to 20 minutes", ["Every 2 to 3 hours", "Every 24 hours", "Every 10 to 20 seconds"],
 "In warm, moist food some bacteria divide every 10 to 20 minutes, so one cell can become millions in a few hours. That is why time out of temperature control matters."),
("How do bacteria multiply?",
 "By dividing in two", ["By laying eggs", "By forming moulds", "By absorbing water"],
 "Bacteria multiply by binary fission: each cell splits into two. They need moisture to grow but do not multiply by absorbing water."),
("What is a bacterial spore?",
 "A tough resting form of bacteria", ["A type of mould found on bread", "A virus carried by raw shellfish", "A toxin made by yeasts in dough"],
 "Some bacteria, such as Bacillus cereus and Clostridium perfringens, form spores that survive normal cooking. They can germinate and grow again if food cools slowly."),
("Why must cooked rice be cooled quickly?",
 "Bacillus cereus can grow", ["Salmonella from the water", "Listeria from the pan", "It loses its starch"],
 "Bacillus cereus spores survive cooking and, if rice is left warm, they germinate and can produce toxins. Cool rice quickly and keep it chilled or hot."),
("What is a bacterial toxin?",
 "A poison made by some bacteria", ["A cleaning chemical left behind", "A type of food allergen", "A virus that grows in food"],
 "Some bacteria produce toxins in food or in the gut. These poisons cause illness even when the bacteria themselves are no longer present."),
("Why can reheating fail to make food safe once bacteria have grown?",
 "Some toxins survive heating", ["Toxins form only in fridges", "Heat turns toxins into spores", "Reheating adds new bacteria"],
 "Reheating kills many bacteria, but some toxins, such as those from Staphylococcus aureus and Bacillus cereus, are heat stable. Prevention through temperature control is the answer."),
("Which bacterium is the most common cause of bacterial food poisoning in the UK and is linked to raw poultry?",
 "Campylobacter", ["Listeria", "Bacillus cereus", "Clostridium botulinum"],
 "Campylobacter is the UK's most commonly reported cause of bacterial food poisoning and is often found on raw chicken. Do not wash raw chicken, as splashing spreads it."),
("Which bacterium is most commonly linked with raw eggs and poultry?",
 "Salmonella", ["Bacillus cereus", "Listeria", "Staphylococcus aureus"],
 "Salmonella is associated with raw eggs, poultry and raw meat. Thorough cooking kills it, and good separation stops it spreading."),
("Which bacterium can grow at fridge temperatures and is especially dangerous in pregnancy?",
 "Listeria", ["Salmonella", "Campylobacter", "Clostridium perfringens"],
 "Listeria can multiply slowly even in the fridge and is linked to pâté, soft cheese and chilled ready-to-eat foods. It can harm unborn babies, so stock rotation and use-by dates matter."),
("Which bacterium is commonly carried in the nose, on skin and in infected cuts?",
 "Staphylococcus aureus", ["E. coli O157", "Clostridium perfringens", "Campylobacter"],
 "Staphylococcus aureus lives on many people's skin and in their noses. Good hand washing and covering cuts stop it reaching food, where it can form heat-stable toxins."),
("Large pots of stew left to cool slowly are linked with which bacterium?",
 "Clostridium perfringens", ["Staphylococcus aureus", "Campylobacter", "Listeria"],
 "Clostridium perfringens forms spores that survive cooking and grow fast as large volumes of meat dishes cool slowly. Divide and cool food quickly."),
("Which bacterium is linked to undercooked minced beef and can cause kidney failure?",
 "E. coli O157", ["Bacillus cereus", "Listeria", "Staphylococcus aureus"],
 "E. coli O157 can cause serious illness, including kidney failure, from a very small dose. Cook burgers and mince thoroughly and keep raw and ready-to-eat food apart."),
("What is norovirus?",
 "A highly infectious virus", ["A bacterium found in rice", "A mould that grows on bread", "A toxin found in oily fish"],
 "Norovirus causes sudden vomiting and diarrhoea and spreads easily from person to person, through surfaces and through food handled by infected people."),
("Can viruses multiply in food?",
 "No, but food can carry them", ["Yes, much faster than bacteria", "Yes, but only when chilled", "Only in very sugary foods"],
 "Viruses need living cells to multiply, so they do not grow in food. Food and surfaces can still carry them to people, which is why ill staff must stay away."),
("Which group is most at risk of serious illness from food poisoning?",
 "Pregnant women", ["Healthy teenagers", "Adults in their 30s", "Professional athletes"],
 "Pregnant women, babies and young children, older people and people who are already ill have the highest risk of serious illness."),
("What are the most common symptoms of food poisoning?",
 "Diarrhoea and vomiting", ["Sneezing and itchy eyes", "Headache and a cough", "Hiccups and dry skin"],
 "Diarrhoea, vomiting, stomach pain, nausea and fever are typical. Food handlers with diarrhoea or vomiting must report it and stay away from work."),
("Which of these is a high-risk food?",
 "Cooked prawns", ["Dry pasta", "Jam", "Pickled onions"],
 "High-risk foods are ready to eat and support bacterial growth: cooked meat and shellfish, dairy, eggs and cooked rice. Dry, sugary or acidic foods are lower risk."),
("Why is dry pasta a low-risk food?",
 "It has too little moisture", ["It is high in protein", "It is stored chilled", "It contains natural preservatives"],
 "Bacteria need moisture to grow, so dry foods are low risk while dry. Once pasta is cooked it becomes moist and needs temperature control."),
("What is cross-contamination?",
 "Bacteria spreading to other food", ["Cooking two different foods together", "Freezing food a second time", "Mixing up the labels on food"],
 "Cross-contamination is the transfer of harmful bacteria, usually from raw food, to ready-to-eat food, directly or through hands, equipment and surfaces."),
("In a fridge, which arrangement is the greatest cross-contamination risk?",
 "Raw meat above cooked meat", ["Cooked meat above raw meat", "Salad above covered raw meat", "Cooked food in lidded tubs"],
 "Raw meat on a higher shelf can drip onto ready-to-eat food below. Store raw meat covered at the bottom, below cooked and ready-to-eat food."),
("Which is an example of indirect cross-contamination?",
 "One knife for raw and cooked", ["Raw meat on the bottom shelf", "Cooked food kept covered", "Hands washed between tasks"],
 "Indirect cross-contamination happens through a vehicle such as a knife, board, cloth or hands. Use separate equipment or clean and disinfect between uses."),
("In UK kitchens, which colour chopping board is usually used for raw meat?",
 "Red", ["Blue", "Yellow", "Green"],
 "The common UK colour code is red for raw meat, blue for raw fish, yellow for cooked meat, green for salad and fruit, brown for vegetables and white for bakery and dairy."),
("In UK kitchens, which colour chopping board is usually used for raw fish?",
 "Blue", ["Red", "Brown", "White"],
 "Blue is used for raw fish in the common colour code. It is good practice rather than law, but it helps keep raw and ready-to-eat foods apart."),
("In UK kitchens, which colour chopping board is usually used for cooked meat?",
 "Yellow", ["Purple", "Green", "Brown"],
 "Yellow boards are used for cooked meat so it never meets a board used for raw meat (red)."),
("In UK kitchens, which colour chopping board is usually used for salad and fruit?",
 "Green", ["Brown", "Blue", "Yellow"],
 "Green is for salad and fruit, which are often eaten raw. Brown is for unwashed vegetables, which may carry soil."),
("How can physical contamination from packaging be reduced?",
 "Unpack away from open food", ["Store boxes on the floor", "Cut the tape over the food", "Reuse old cardboard boxes"],
 "Remove outer packaging such as cardboard, staples and string away from open food. Cardboard also brings in dirt and pests."),
("Which practice reduces the risk of chemical contamination?",
 "Store chemicals away from food", ["Decant chemicals into food tubs", "Use extra chemical to be safe", "Keep chemicals on food shelves"],
 "Keep chemicals in their own labelled containers in a separate store and follow the dilution instructions. Food tubs and food shelves invite mistakes."),
("Why must cleaning chemicals stay in their original containers?",
 "So they are always labelled", ["So they keep their strength", "So they last much longer", "So they mix better with water"],
 "The original container carries the name, hazard warnings and instructions. Chemicals in unlabelled or food containers have been mistaken for food or drink."),
("A loaf of bread has a patch of mould. What should you do?",
 "Throw the whole loaf away", ["Cut off the mouldy part", "Toast it to kill the mould", "Freeze it and use it later"],
 "Mould roots can spread through soft foods like bread further than you can see, and some moulds make toxins. Discard the whole item."),
("Damaged or blown cans should be rejected. Which rare but serious bacterium is the concern?",
 "Clostridium botulinum", ["Campylobacter", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Salmonella"],
 "Clostridium botulinum can grow without air, for example in faulty cans, and produces a very dangerous toxin. Blown, badly dented or leaking cans are thrown away."),
("How do spoilage organisms differ from food poisoning bacteria?",
 "They make food look or smell off", ["They cause most cases of food poisoning", "They cannot grow in warm food", "They only grow on raw meat"],
 "Spoilage organisms change the smell, taste or look of food. Pathogens that cause food poisoning usually do not, so food can look normal and still be unsafe."),
("Can you tell by looking whether food contains food poisoning bacteria?",
 "No, it often looks normal", ["Yes, it turns a grey colour", "Yes, it smells sour", "Only if it feels slimy"],
 "Harmful bacteria rarely change the look, smell or taste of food. Temperature control, separation and use-by dates are the protection, not inspection by eye."),
]
